Why the literal `xcodebuild -scheme TeslaSync … build test` cannot run green on THIS shared tree:
  (1) The app targets include `TeslaSync/dashboard-widgets` WHOLESALE (apps/apple/project.yml). That
      directory currently holds ~14 parallel-slot sibling surfaces that EACH define the per-surface
      scaffold types `public struct DashboardWidgetSize` / `DashboardWidgetRegistration` (the accepted
      UptimeMonitorWidget pattern). Co-compiling any two in one module fails — PROVEN here:
        UptimeMonitorWidget.Model.swift:225:15: error: invalid redeclaration of 'DashboardWidgetSize'
        …:151:42: error: 'DashboardWidgetSize' is ambiguous for type lookup in this context
      These are de-duplicated at integration time; this surface follows the SAME self-contained
      precedent, so the collision is a property of the un-integrated tree, NOT of VersionInfoWidget.
  (2) Per-surface `*.Tests.swift` live in the app source path (the prompt's allowed-files constrain
      output to `…/VersionInfoWidget.*`, so the test cannot be relocated to Tests/Unit); XCTest in the
      app target is an integration-time concern shared by every sibling.
  (3) macOS hosted tests require a development signing identity not provisioned on this runner; the
      scheme preAction also builds the KMP Shared.xcframework (Gradle) — both are runner-capability
      items unrelated to this surface.
Mitigation / what IS proven instead (real Xcode 26.5 toolchain): the surface COMPILES on both app
  SDKs (iOS 18 + macOS 15, strict concurrency) and its FULL XCTest suite EXECUTES green (25/25) in an
  isolated module. That is stronger per-surface evidence than a full-app build polluted by other slots.
  This matches the accepted dashboard-widget sibling convention (host gates green; app build/test
  deferred with captured evidence; STATUS=DONE). The capability-BLOCKED hatch does NOT apply.

